{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Game Pacing.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Game Pacing.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"game pacing game pacing","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Game Pacing.md","fileName":"Game Pacing.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Game Pacing","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 14:36\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Game Balance and Progression]] [[Level and Scene Design]] [[Game Challenge Design]] [[Level Layout and Progression]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Balance and Progression","Level and Scene Design","Game Challenge Design","Level Layout and Progression","Progression Curve","Game Scene","Scene Shape","advancedgamedesign.pdf","agamedesignvocabulary.pdf","fundamentalsofgamedesign3e.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Game Pacing.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Game Pacing.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"game pacing game pacing","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Game Pacing.md","fileName":"Game Pacing.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Game Pacing","heading":"Game Pacing","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Game pacing is the rate and rhythm at which activity, challenge, reward, and progression unfold. It can be measured through actions, completed challenges, attention, or elapsed time rather than through levels alone.\n\nPacing balances early success with lasting goals and alternates intensity to avoid fatigue. [[Progression Curve|Progression curves]] set the broad rate, while nested interaction loops and playtests reveal how that rate feels to the player.\n\nWithin a [[Game Scene]], pacing can emerge as the [[Scene Shape|shape of choice]] widens and narrows. Ordering scenes determines when rules are introduced, revisited, combined, or inverted, so rhythm can change without continually adding new mechanics.\n\nWithin a level, layout, enemy placement, resources, traversal time, story events, and safe areas determine the rate of demand and recovery. Varying that rate keeps sustained intensity from becoming exhausting and keeps quiet stretches from becoming monotonous.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Balance and Progression","Level and Scene Design","Game Challenge Design","Level Layout and Progression","Progression Curve","Game Scene","Scene Shape","advancedgamedesign.pdf","agamedesignvocabulary.pdf","fundamentalsofgamedesign3e.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Game Pacing.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Game Pacing.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"game pacing game pacing","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Game Pacing.md","fileName":"Game Pacing.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Game Pacing","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[advancedgamedesign.pdf]]\n[[agamedesignvocabulary.pdf]]\n\n[[fundamentalsofgamedesign3e.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Balance and Progression","Level and Scene Design","Game Challenge Design","Level Layout and Progression","Progression Curve","Game Scene","Scene Shape","advancedgamedesign.pdf","agamedesignvocabulary.pdf","fundamentalsofgamedesign3e.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}